WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.150
In this place so unreal

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.820
I wait for dawn to come

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:12.950
Farther and farther, like the sky I saw that day

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.210
What a joke this hope is

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.750
I acted as if I had forgotten

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:28.260
Farther and farther, like the dream I saw that day

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:34.140
I just want to know the meaning of it

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.270
Within this scattered time

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.100
Along with the breath of ours

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.070
And of this pain that never goes away

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.570
Even though the roaring of the sea muffles the voices

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.490
Darkness engulfs everything ahead

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.490
The light that passes through our fragile hearts

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.910
Won't go away

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.750
We only relied on our compass

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.880
We believed you

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:14.760
While we stand here

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:19.890
We're searching for the unseen future

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:29.860
Zipang

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.530
Hold on, Yosuke.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.740
Aren't you afraid?

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.450
You're gonna be messing history with your own hands?

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:45.080
I'm neither a ghost or a butterfly.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.210
I'm here as an SDF officer.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.860
Drifters

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.210
The cockpit's been heavily targeted. It's totally riddled...

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.510
The pilot seems to be dead.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:42.220
And the man in the white uniform at the back appears to be dead, too.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.890
We don't know for sure! Situation report!

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.600
Roger! November. This is November Two.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.610
Of the two men crew, we've confirmed the death of the pilot.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.190
A second man in uniform is in the rear seat. His condition is unclear.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.900
We are moving in now to further assess the situation.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.990
What's wrong?

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:04.950
The plane is starting to sink!

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.160
The wings must have filled with water!

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.710
Executive Officer! What are you...?!

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.300
The Executive Commander has jumped in!

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.850
Masayuki... I'm an SDF official.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.320
I have no hesitations when it comes to saving a person's life.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.320
Our Executive Officer has done it again.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.410
This is his third rescue, right?

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.870
His top priority should be to assess the situation.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.870
What he's doing is totally unacceptable.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.910
Oh well, he's not the kind of guy who waits around for instructions.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.080
He's a man of action who takes his own initiative.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.380
The Executive Officer has resurfaced with the survivor!

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.010
Report to the bridge. One survivor has been rescued!

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.640
One survivor has been rescued!

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.890
Roger that. Assess his condition and report back.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.520
The pilot and the plane have both sunk.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:20.230
The survivor is unconscious and in critical condition.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:24.410
Items recovered include a military sword, and a black leather bag.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:27.870
Judging from his epaulets, he should be a Lieutenant Commander.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.040
Permission given for on-board medical treatment.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.750
Bring him in.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.750
Roger.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.130
Year 2000... no.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:48.430
Year 1942, June 5th, 1930 hrs.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.270
Latitude, 30 degrees 59 north.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.850
Longitude 178 degrees 30 east, at sea.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:05:00.070
A naval officer has been rescued from a plane wreck.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.820
We have all...

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.450
...stepped into history.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.080
It's Lieutenant Momoi.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.710
Come on in.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.590
Thank you.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.050
What is the condition of the man we rescued?

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.100
Well, there are no bullet wounds or external injuries on him,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.730
but due to the crash impact during the water landing,

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.560
he seems to have three fractured ribs,

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:40.820
a slight sprain to the neck, and a bruising on his forehead.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:45.150
His blood pressure is still very low and his pulse is weak.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.490
He requires absolute rest for the time being.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:49.950
The fractured areas are set in place,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.830
and he is placed on intravenous infusion.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.830
As he is now on painkillers,

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.130
it will take some time before he regains consciousness.

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:02.840
In that case, a full recovery will take more than a month?

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:05.550
Yes, I think he needs about two months for that.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.340
Let us move on to the main issue.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:11.850
The spare room in the sickbay that you are in-charge of...

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.060
I would like that man to be moved there.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.850
Yes, sir. But, what about my personal belongings?

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.060
I'd like you to remove everything.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.730
Including all the newspapers and magazines.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.360
During his recovery, no information...

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.030
...about this ship, or any of its crew, is to be given to that man.

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:31.740
The only person allowed to have direct contact with him...

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.950
...is you, First Lieutenant Momoi.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.910
Are you saying he's to be confined to that room?

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.210
That man is a soldier. And we're in the midst of a war.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.170
It's only appropriate.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.420
I understand. I shall begin at once.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.920
I apologize for the inconvenience.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:52.350
No, not at all! What's more...

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.930
He looks like a nice man, and kind of good-looking too!

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.570
No way. Two whole months!

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.400
To withhold all information for that long is just not possible.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.950
I say, we should just exchange information.

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:15.620
We should get all the information we need to know...

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.540
...while we're in this era, am I right?

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:23.080
If he finds out about the ship's capabilities, we can't allow him to leave.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:24.960
Would you like to bear that responsibility, Oguri?

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.510
Since we've rescued him, we'll have to do what we can.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.090
I will bear full responsibility.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.520
Imagine waking up in a ship, 60 years into the future.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.100
It's not something you'd see in your wildest dreams.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:43.150
It might've been better for him to have drowned instead.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.400
Hey, the door was locked so I couldn't get inside.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:51.450
Still feeling a little seasick, so I thought I'd get an aspirin.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:55.370
Oh no, you don't! Get out on the deck and get some fresh air instead!

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:59.370
How does an Imperial Soldier 60 years back look like?

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.250
Come on, just one picture!

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.750
He's asleep, so what harm could taking one picture do?

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:26.940
It's so dark and cold in here. And... so quiet.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.900
At this rate, I'll probably...

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.360
This sound...

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:36.160
What amazing strength...

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.580
Just who are you?

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:41.500
Just who... are you?

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:50.460
If you keep hanging around here, I'm gonna throw your camera...

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:53.760
...into the sea for the sharks!
- Don't be so mean!

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.090
It's the Captain's orders!

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.720
No one's allowed!

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.530
H-He's awake?

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.530
So is this... heaven?

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.530
I guess not.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.200
It must be hell then.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:25.750
That's not it either. You are still alive.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:29.290
Right now, you shouldn't worry about a thing, and just continue to sleep.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.920
The slight rocking... I'm on board a ship?

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.590
I am...

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.850
Still alive...

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:05.250
Why am I panicking like a little girl!

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:09.120
There's no time to waste.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:55.670
He's gone? What do you mean?

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:58.800
I was clearing out my belongings and took my eyes off him for a second...

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:02.300
He took his sword, his bag, and his uniform along with him.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:05.600
I got it. He couldn't have gotten far. Go check the corridors.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.980
Captain, the soldier we rescued has escaped.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.230
He's regained consciousness?

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.150
We should've confiscated his military sword.

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:16.360
He might attempt suicide.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:21.700
CIC, check the surveillance monitors in all areas.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.950
The casualty we rescued is on the loose.

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:25.200
Aye, sir!

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:27.870
Attention all hands.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:31.540
Casualty is at large aboard the ship, carrying a military sword.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:33.830
Do not approach.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:38.760
I'll go get him.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.510
Executive Officer, should we retrieve some pistols?

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:44.720
We don't need something like that. That man's pretty badly injured!

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:48.810
He won't be able to resist us! He must be somewhere on this deck!

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:51.940
- Executive Officer!
- Have you found him?

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.690
Over there.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:09.950
This ship is...

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:12.960
So beautiful.

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.540
It's very different from our Imperial Navy Fleet.

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.420
Or from all the Allied ships.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.260
You've severe injuries and really need to be back in the sickbay.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:33.060
I will complete my mission, no matter how far I have to swim.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:36.770
If my strength fails me, I'll go meet my fallen comrades.

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:40.110
This man here saved your life!

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:42.030
He plunged into the sea alone!

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:53.410
I had planned to swim ashore from here,

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:56.420
but the sea breeze has reminded me that,

00:12:56.420 --> 00:13:00.670
even though I'm in the navy, I'm really not a good swimmer.

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:15.690
I'd like to know this ship's allegiance and its mission.

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:21.320
Unfortunately...

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:24.110
I cannot tell you that.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:28.450
Why we are here, and where we are heading...

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:30.580
We'd really like to know ourselves.

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:32.790
So are you an enemy?

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:34.580
Or an ally?

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:38.540
I can only tell you that...

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:41.630
All of us on board, are Japanese, just like you.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.380
My name is Yosuke Kadomatsu.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:01.070
Lieutenant commander Takumi Kusaka.

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:03.400
And for saving my life...

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:05.650
You have my humble gratitude.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:17.330
Lieutenant Commander Kusaka.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:20.290
Is that what he said? Well, thank you.

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:24.170
We'll be back at starting point in a few minutes. Be on standby until then.

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:28.840
I wonder if something will happen.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:31.760
We could not detect the low atmospheric pressure...

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:33.470
...that we had when we arrived.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:36.520
However, if there was a dimensional glitch,

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:39.230
I wonder if it's still around somewhere?

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:44.110
- When that occurs...
- What will happen to that fleet?

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:49.610
73 miles ahead of us, there's a huge fleet retreating back to Japan.

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:52.530
If we manage to escape from here...

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:56.790
They should disappear from our radar.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:58.290
Just like the rest of our fleet.

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:01.460
Well then, what about Lieutenant Commander Kusaka?

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:04.040
I don't know.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:07.260
I'm guessing something strange might happen to that man.

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:14.470
Alright, all engines stop. Shut down the main engines.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.450
Oh please! Please let us go back!

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:36.580
Back to Heisei Period Japan!

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:25.670
The ship has stopped?

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.170
Is there a reason?

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.900
- What's happening?
- Are we back yet?

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:57.950
- Has the moon changed?
- We'll know out on the deck!

00:16:57.950 --> 00:16:59.870
I'm coming along!

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:07.330
Why? Why is nothing happening?!

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:11.300
What happened to the clouds? When's the low air pressure coming?

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:13.510
Any changes?

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:16.390
No changes on the radar display monitor.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:20.260
As I've guessed... we're still here.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:24.140
Something is different from the time we arrived here.

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:28.020
What is it, sir?

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:32.860
It's him! That soldier wasn't with us when we first came here!

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:36.240
The conditions are no longer the same!

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:39.410
And that's why, we are unable to go back!

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:41.740
But then, he's only just one person.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:43.580
There's no other reason that I can think of!

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:47.830
There must be some kind of explanation, for us all to be stuck here!

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.340
What's wrong, Kashiwabara? Go back to the cabin if you're not on duty.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:56.720
You're off duty.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:00.050
Before I do that, the man owes me an explanation!

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:03.390
What are you saying?! This man here is seriously injured.

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.020
The captain's orders are that no one is to speak with him!

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.310
Interesting.

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:12.770
Who's the one who didn't hear the captain and Executive Officer's orders?

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.530
It's okay.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.990
I'll just speak my mind!

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:20.820
You were supposed to die on that seaplane!

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.080
You're a man who shouldn't exist in history at all!

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:26.080
In... history?

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:31.290
- Stop it! Kashiwabara!
- But it's the truth, isn't it?

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.590
Our ship had no part in that battle.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:36.300
The fact that you're still alive is the cause of all the problems!

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:39.340
It's because you're here that all of us can't go back!

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:55.650
Starboard ahead, target has come to a complete stop.

00:18:56.150 --> 00:19:01.240
It's probably that crippled ship that broke away from the Japanese Fleet.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:03.660
It's easy pickings.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:05.620
Take us up to periscope depth.

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:20.590
Target, course 320, 3600 yards.

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:24.350
There is no smoke from the stack, the boiler must be out.

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:27.350
Its engine is probably seriously damaged.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:31.900
From the structure of its bridge, it should be of Takao-class.

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:35.940
No armaments aft, just a huge sea plane hangar.

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:40.110
A new vessel from the Japanese Navy.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:44.530
The human mind often dwells in its imagination.

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:48.080
The man-made wings of Leonardo da Vinci...

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:52.370
...are a symbol of man's imagination during the European Renaissance.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:56.170
All of you here are no doubt, Japanese.

00:19:56.340 --> 00:20:00.840
However, you are not the same Japanese that I am.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:05.390
This ship here was not built by our Imperial Navy.

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:08.810
It's a creation inspired by some others.

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:13.060
You are no doubt Japanese, but where have you come from?

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.020
If I were to tell you that...

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:19.530
You might never be able to get back to your homeland again.

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:25.320
You will become a drifter of this Pacific Ocean together with us all!

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:35.920
Anyone who boards a ship should be mentally prepared for that.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:37.420
I'll tell you then!

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:40.340
Even though you're not gonna believe what I say! We are...

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:46.600
We...

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:53.600
...came from the Japan of the 21st Century.

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:11.540
Ready tubes one and two!

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:15.580
Aye, Sir! Ready tubes one and two.

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:20.550
Tubes one and two, loaded in! Opening hatch.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:29.300
Don't blame me.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:32.680
Blame your bad luck for having stopped your ship in a place like this.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:39.770
So that means, in that era...

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:44.990
Japan still exists?

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:51.080
Yes.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:05.720
Tubes one and two, ready to fire.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:09.680
Fire!

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:17.210
Passing through the distant time

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:22.450
Just peacefully

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:34.260
Without losing your way, you walked towards me

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:47.140
How many times have I grasped tightly those small hands of yours?

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:53.750
Not being able to do anything

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:59.820
I kept on praying

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:11.670
Sorrow or agony isn't enough

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:27.350
That's why now as if I wanted to make up... I'm looking at you

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:44.260
To all of us here, is the enemy really America?

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:47.640
Right now, we have to accept the situation as it is.

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:51.650
However, to sink a ship that isn't meant to be sunk in history,

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:53.570
will mean there's no turning back for us.

00:23:53.570 --> 00:23:56.400
Is this part of God's plan? Or a will of the devil?

00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:59.650
This is, a battle of the 21st century.

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:04.960
